(A map holds values under text keys and keeps them in the order they were
 added. Looking up a missing key raises the error that "on error" catches.
 A map passed as a value keeps its type through the call, so it still
 prints as a map.)

To 'print the record' with a value called record.
    Print record.

A map called person is {"name": "Ada", "age": 36, "city": "London"}.

Print "The record: {person}".

A text called name is person's "name".
Print "The name is {name}.".

Set person's "age" to 37.
A number called age is person's "age".
Print "After a birthday the age is {age}, and the record holds {person's length} entries.".

Print "Keys:".
For each key in person's keys,
    print key.

Print "Values:".
For each detail in person's values,
    print detail.

A map called nested is {"tags": ["a", "b"], "meta": {"id": 1}}.
Print "Nested: {nested}".
A list called tags is nested's "tags".
Print "Tags: {tags}".
A map called meta is nested's "meta".
A number called id is meta's "id".
Print "The meta id is {id}.".

Print "The record, printed by a function that takes any value:".
'print the record' of person.

Print person's "nope".
On error print "Looked up a missing key.".
